// Java Fundamentals — Part 1 (q801–910)
window.QUIZ_BANK['java'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['java']||[]).concat([
{id:801,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is Java\'s "write once, run anywhere" principle enabled by?',options:['The Java compiler producing native machine code','The Java Virtual Machine (JVM) executing bytecode on any platform that has a compatible JVM installed','The Java Runtime Environment compiling source code at runtime','Java programs being distributed as source code'],answer:1,explanation:'Java compiles source to platform-independent bytecode (.class files). The JVM on the target platform interprets or JIT-compiles this bytecode, achieving portability.'},
{id:802,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What are the four pillars of Object-Oriented Programming in Java?',options:['Abstraction, Polymorphism, Encapsulation, Inheritance','Classes, Objects, Methods, Variables','Interfaces, Enums, Arrays, Lists','Compilation, Interpretation, Execution, Debugging'],answer:0,explanation:'The four OOP pillars: Encapsulation (data hiding), Inheritance (code reuse via is-a), Polymorphism (one interface, multiple implementations), Abstraction (hiding complexity behind clean interfaces).'},
{id:803,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between "==" and ".equals()" in Java?',options:['They are always identical','== compares object references (memory addresses); .equals() compares object content/state by default or overridden logic','== compares strings only; .equals() compares all objects','== is for primitives; .equals() is for strings only'],answer:1,explanation:'== checks reference equality (same object in memory). .equals() checks logical equality (same value). For String: "hello" == "hello" may be true (string pool) or false (new String("hello")); "hello".equals("hello") is always true.'},
{id:804,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a "constructor" in Java?',options:['A method that destroys an object when called','A special method with the same name as the class, no return type, called automatically when an object is created with "new"','A method that copies an object','A static method that creates class instances'],answer:1,explanation:'A constructor initialises a new object: same name as the class, no return type (not even void). If no constructor is defined, Java provides a default no-arg constructor.'},
{id:805,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is "inheritance" in Java?',options:['Copying code from one class to another','A mechanism where a class (subclass) extends another class (superclass), inheriting its non-private fields and methods and optionally overriding them','Implementing multiple interfaces in one class','Importing libraries into a Java class'],answer:1,explanation:'Inheritance (extends keyword) creates an is-a relationship: the subclass inherits accessible members of the superclass and can override methods or add new ones.'},
{id:806,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does the "final" keyword mean when applied to a variable?',options:['The variable is static','The variable\'s value cannot be changed after initialisation — it becomes a constant','The variable is only accessible within the class','The variable is automatically garbage collected'],answer:1,explanation:'final on a variable: primitive types cannot change value; reference types cannot be re-assigned to a different object (but the object\'s state can still be modified).'},
{id:807,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does "static" mean in Java?',options:['The variable or method is always final','The member belongs to the class rather than to any specific instance — shared across all instances, accessible via the class name','The method cannot be overridden','The variable is stored in a fixed memory location'],answer:1,explanation:'static members belong to the class: static variables are shared (one copy), static methods can be called without creating an object (ClassName.method()), and cannot access instance members directly.'},
{id:808,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is "encapsulation" in Java?',options:['Using multiple classes in one file','The practice of hiding internal data (private fields) and exposing controlled access through public getter/setter methods — protecting object integrity','Wrapping primitives in wrapper classes','Encrypting class variables'],answer:1,explanation:'Encapsulation bundles data and methods together and restricts direct access: private fields, public getters/setters allow controlled access while hiding implementation details.'},
{id:809,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is an "interface" in Java?',options:['A class that cannot be instantiated','A contract that defines a set of abstract methods (and optionally default/static methods from Java 8+) that implementing classes must provide','A final class with no methods','A class that extends Object only'],answer:1,explanation:'An interface defines a contract: abstract method signatures that implementing classes must provide. From Java 8, interfaces can also contain default (with body) and static methods.'},
{id:810,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is an "abstract class" in Java?',options:['A class with only static methods','A class that cannot be instantiated directly, may contain abstract methods (without body) that subclasses must implement, and may also contain concrete methods','A class that implements all interfaces','A class with only final methods'],answer:1,explanation:'Abstract classes provide a partial implementation: they may have abstract methods (subclasses must implement) and concrete methods (inherited as-is). Cannot be instantiated with new.'},
{id:811,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is "polymorphism" in Java?',options:['Having multiple classes with the same name','The ability for a reference variable of a supertype (class or interface) to hold objects of different subtypes, with method calls resolving to the actual object\'s implementation at runtime','Implementing multiple interfaces in one class','Overloading methods with the same name'],answer:1,explanation:'Polymorphism: one interface, multiple implementations. A Shape reference can hold Circle or Rectangle objects; calling draw() invokes the appropriate override dynamically (runtime polymorphism).'},
{id:812,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the Java "main" method signature?',options:['public void main(String args)','public static void main(String[] args)','static public void main(String args[])','Both B and C are valid signatures'],answer:3,explanation:'Both "public static void main(String[] args)" and "public static void main(String args[])" are valid; from Java 21 instance main methods are also supported. The canonical form is public static void main(String[] args).'},
{id:813,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What are Java\'s primitive data types?',options:['int, String, double, boolean','byte, short, int, long, float, double, char, boolean','Integer, Long, Float, Double, Character, Boolean','int, double, string, object'],answer:1,explanation:'Java has 8 primitives: byte (8-bit), short (16-bit), int (32-bit), long (64-bit), float (32-bit float), double (64-bit float), char (16-bit Unicode), boolean (true/false).'},
{id:814,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is "autoboxing" in Java?',options:['Automatically creating backup copies of objects','The automatic conversion between primitive types (int, double) and their corresponding wrapper classes (Integer, Double) by the Java compiler','Automatically compressing objects in memory','Casting objects to their supertype automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'Autoboxing: int → Integer, double → Double, etc. automatically by the compiler. Unboxing: Integer → int. Enables using primitives in generic collections (List<Integer>).'},
{id:815,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a "NullPointerException" (NPE) in Java?',options:['An exception when array indices are out of bounds','A runtime exception thrown when trying to use a null reference to call a method, access a field, or perform any operation that requires a non-null object','An exception caused by dividing by zero','An exception when casting incompatible types'],answer:1,explanation:'NPE is thrown when code attempts to dereference a null reference: null.method(), null.field, array[i] when array is null. Java 14+ NullPointerExceptions include helpful messages identifying the null operand.'},
{id:816,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between "method overloading" and "method overriding"?',options:['They are identical','Overloading: same method name in the same class with different parameter lists (compile-time polymorphism). Overriding: subclass provides a new implementation for a method inherited from the superclass (runtime polymorphism)','Overriding is only for static methods','Overloading requires the @Override annotation'],answer:1,explanation:'Overloading resolves at compile time based on parameter types/count. Overriding resolves at runtime based on the actual object type. Overloading is ad-hoc polymorphism; overriding is subtype polymorphism.'},
{id:817,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "super" keyword used for in Java?',options:['To call a final method in the parent class','To call the superclass constructor (super()), access overridden superclass methods (super.method()), or access hidden superclass fields (super.field)','To create a new instance of the superclass','To cast an object to its supertype'],answer:1,explanation:'super() calls the parent constructor (must be first statement in child constructor). super.method() calls the parent\'s version of an overridden method. super.field accesses a hidden parent field.'},
{id:818,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "this" keyword in Java?',options:['A reference to the current class definition','A reference to the current object instance, used to disambiguate fields from parameters, call other constructors within the same class (this()), or pass the current object as a parameter','A static reference to the class','A keyword for accessing thread-local storage'],answer:1,explanation:'this refers to the current instance: this.field disambiguates field from local variable with same name; this() calls another constructor; return this enables method chaining.'},
{id:819,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What are "checked" vs "unchecked" exceptions in Java?',options:['Checked exceptions are errors; unchecked are warnings','Checked exceptions (extend Exception but not RuntimeException) must be declared in method signatures (throws) or handled (try-catch); unchecked exceptions (extend RuntimeException) do not require declaration or handling','Unchecked exceptions cannot be caught','Checked exceptions are from the JVM; unchecked from user code'],answer:1,explanation:'Checked: IOException, SQLException — compiler enforces declaration/handling. Unchecked: RuntimeException subclasses (NPE, IllegalArgumentException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException) — no mandatory handling but can be caught.'},
{id:820,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "try-with-resources" statement in Java?',options:['A try block that catches all resources (files, connections) that are thrown','A Java 7+ construct that automatically closes resources implementing AutoCloseable when the try block exits — eliminating the need for finally blocks to close resources and preventing resource leaks','A way to try multiple exception handlers simultaneously','A try block that retries on exception'],answer:1,explanation:'try (Resource r = new Resource()) {} — r.close() is automatically called when the block exits (normally or with exception), even before any catch. Multiple resources can be declared.'},
{id:821,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a "lambda expression" in Java?',options:['A mathematical lambda calculation','A concise way (Java 8+) to represent a function (implementation of a functional interface) as an inline expression: (params) -> expression or (params) -> { statements; }','A type of anonymous class','A thread that executes concurrently'],answer:1,explanation:'Lambdas implement functional interfaces (single abstract method) inline: (x) -> x * 2 implements Function<Integer,Integer>. They enable functional programming patterns, stream pipelines, and cleaner event handler code.'},
{id:822,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Java Stream API used for?',options:['Network I/O streams','A Java 8+ functional-style API for processing sequences of elements with operations like filter(), map(), reduce(), collect() — enabling declarative, potentially parallel processing of collections and other data sources','Reading bytes from files','Thread synchronisation'],answer:1,explanation:'Java Streams support pipeline operations: source.filter(pred).map(func).collect(collector) or .reduce(). Lazy evaluation, optional parallelism (parallelStream()), and rich terminal operations for aggregation.'},
{id:823,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is an "Optional" in Java (Java 8+)?',options:['An optional import statement','A container that may or may not hold a non-null value, explicitly modelling the possibility of absence instead of using null — avoiding NullPointerExceptions and forcing explicit null handling','An optional parameter in a method signature','A nullable annotation'],answer:1,explanation:'Optional<T> wraps a potentially-null value; methods like isPresent(), orElse(), orElseThrow(), map(), ifPresent() make null handling explicit and functional. Never use Optional as a field type or parameter.'},
{id:824,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "generics" in Java?',options:['A general-purpose class hierarchy','Type parameters enabling type-safe, reusable code by specifying the type to be used at compile time: List<String>, Map<String, Integer> — preventing ClassCastException and eliminating casts','A runtime type-checking mechanism','A way to write code that works on any Object'],answer:1,explanation:'Generics provide compile-time type safety for collections and algorithms: List<String> only accepts Strings, catching type mismatches at compile time. Type erasure removes generic info at runtime.'},
{id:825,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "type erasure" in Java generics?',options:['Deleting type declarations from source code','The JVM mechanism by which generic type parameters are removed (erased) at compile time, replaced with their upper bounds (Object or bounded type), enabling backward compatibility with pre-generics code','Erasing the type information from objects at runtime','Converting all generics to Object at compile time'],answer:1,explanation:'Type erasure: List<String> and List<Integer> both become List at runtime. Generic type parameters are checked at compile time only; at runtime, all type information is erased. This prevents creating arrays of generic types.'},
{id:826,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between ArrayList and LinkedList in Java?',options:['They are identical','ArrayList: backed by a resizable array (O(1) random access, O(n) insert/delete in middle); LinkedList: doubly-linked list (O(n) random access, O(1) insert/delete at ends/known position with iterator)','LinkedList is always faster','ArrayList does not support generics'],answer:1,explanation:'ArrayList: fast random access (index), slower middle insert/delete (shift). LinkedList: slow random access (traverse), fast front/back insertion, implements Deque. ArrayList is preferred for most use cases.'},
{id:827,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between HashMap and TreeMap in Java?',options:['HashMap allows null keys; TreeMap does not allow any entries','HashMap: hash-based (O(1) average get/put, no order); TreeMap: red-black tree (O(log n) get/put, keys sorted in natural or Comparator order)','TreeMap is always faster','HashMap is thread-safe; TreeMap is not'],answer:1,explanation:'HashMap: fastest for get/put, no ordering. TreeMap: sorted by key (natural order or custom Comparator), enables firstKey(), lastKey(), subMap() range operations at O(log n) cost.'},
{id:828,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "HashMap vs Hashtable" in Java?',options:['They are identical','HashMap: not synchronised (not thread-safe), allows one null key and null values, faster; Hashtable: synchronised (thread-safe), no null keys/values, legacy class — replaced by ConcurrentHashMap for thread-safe use','Hashtable is faster','HashMap does not allow null values'],answer:1,explanation:'HashMap is preferred; Hashtable is legacy (all methods synchronised = low concurrency). For thread-safety, use ConcurrentHashMap (segment-level locking, much better concurrency than Hashtable).'},
{id:829,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a "functional interface" in Java?',options:['An interface that contains only functions','An interface with exactly one abstract method (SAM — Single Abstract Method), making it a target for lambda expressions and method references. Examples: Runnable, Callable, Comparator, Function, Predicate, Consumer, Supplier','An interface that is functionally complete','An interface containing default methods only'],answer:1,explanation:'Functional interfaces (annotated with @FunctionalInterface) have exactly one abstract method; they can be instantiated with lambdas or method references. @FunctionalInterface is optional but causes a compile error if more than one abstract method exists.'},
{id:830,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the @Override annotation do in Java?',options:['Forces a method to be called first at class initialisation','Informs the compiler that the method is intended to override a superclass method, causing a compile-time error if the method name or signature does not match any method in the supertype','Overrides all other annotations on the method','Marks the method as final after overriding'],answer:1,explanation:'@Override is a marker annotation: the compiler verifies the method actually overrides a superclass/interface method. Without it, a typo in the method name would silently create a new method instead of overriding.'},
{id:831,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "garbage collection" in Java?',options:['Deleting unused source code files','The JVM\'s automatic memory management that identifies and reclaims memory occupied by objects that are no longer reachable from any live reference, preventing memory leaks without explicit deallocation','A manual process of deleting objects with delete()','The process of removing null references from collections'],answer:1,explanation:'Java\'s garbage collector (G1, ZGC, Shenandoah, etc.) automatically frees memory from unreachable objects. Developers cannot directly trigger GC (System.gc() is a hint only) but can influence it through object lifecycle management.'},
{id:832,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "multithreading" in Java?',options:['Using multiple CPU cores by default','Executing multiple threads (lightweight processes) concurrently within a single JVM process, enabling parallelism, responsiveness, and efficient utilisation of multi-core processors','Using multiple JVM instances simultaneously','Creating multiple classes in one file'],answer:1,explanation:'Java threads (extending Thread or implementing Runnable/Callable) run concurrently. The JVM schedules threads on available CPU cores. Concurrency utilities (ExecutorService, CompletableFuture) are preferred over raw threads.'},
{id:833,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a "synchronized" method in Java?',options:['A method that runs exactly on schedule','A method (or block) that acquires a monitor lock on the associated object before execution, ensuring only one thread can execute it at a time — preventing race conditions on shared mutable state','A method that synchronises data with a database','A method that runs synchronously (blocking)'],answer:1,explanation:'synchronized ensures mutual exclusion: one thread at a time holds the monitor lock on the object (instance method) or class (static method), preventing simultaneous execution of synchronised methods on the same object.'},
{id:834,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "volatile" keyword in Java?',options:['A variable that changes frequently','Guarantees that a variable\'s value is always read from and written to main memory (not CPU cache), ensuring visibility of changes across all threads — prevents stale cached reads but does NOT guarantee atomicity for compound operations','A variable that cannot be garbage collected','A thread-local variable'],answer:1,explanation:'volatile ensures memory visibility: writes are immediately visible to all threads. It prevents stale cache reads but does not make compound operations (check-then-act, i++) atomic — for those, use AtomicInteger or synchronized.'},
{id:835,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between "Comparable" and "Comparator" in Java?',options:['Comparable compares strings; Comparator compares objects','Comparable (java.lang): implemented by the class itself to define natural ordering (compareTo()); Comparator (java.util): separate comparison strategy (compare()) passed externally, allowing multiple orderings for the same class','Comparator is faster','Comparable is for primitives; Comparator for objects'],answer:1,explanation:'Comparable: classes implement it to define their natural sort order (used by Collections.sort, Arrays.sort without explicit Comparator). Comparator: external strategy pattern enabling multiple sort orderings without modifying the class.'},
{id:836,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is Java\'s "memory model" (JMM) and why does it matter for concurrency?',options:['The maximum heap size a JVM can use','The specification defining how threads interact through memory: visibility rules (when writes become visible to other threads), atomicity guarantees, and ordering constraints — critical for writing correct concurrent programs','A model for physical hardware memory allocation','The layout of objects in heap memory'],answer:1,explanation:'JMM defines: actions (read/write, lock/unlock), synchronisation order, happens-before relationships. Without understanding JMM, concurrent code may have data races, stale reads, or unexpected reordering due to CPU/compiler optimisations.'},
{id:837,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What are "Java records" (introduced in Java 16)?',options:['Logging records in Java','Immutable data carrier classes with automatically generated constructors, toString, equals, hashCode, and accessor methods, defined concisely: record Point(int x, int y) {}','A Java database record type','An annotation for immutable fields'],answer:1,explanation:'Records (Java 16+) are immutable data aggregates: the compiler generates a canonical constructor, accessors (x(), y()), equals(), hashCode(), toString() from the component list — eliminating boilerplate for value objects.'},
{id:838,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What are "sealed classes" in Java (Java 17+)?',options:['Classes with all private methods','Classes that restrict which other classes can extend or implement them using the "permits" clause — enabling exhaustive type checking in pattern matching and more controlled class hierarchies','Classes with no public constructors','Classes that are final and cannot be extended'],answer:1,explanation:'Sealed classes (sealed class Shape permits Circle, Rectangle) restrict extension to a defined set of classes, enabling the compiler to check exhaustiveness of instanceof pattern matching — key for algebraic data types.'},
{id:839,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "pattern matching for instanceof" in Java (Java 16+)?',options:['Regular expression matching on class names','The ability to test and cast in one operation: if (obj instanceof String s) { s.length() } — eliminating the explicit cast after instanceof check and making null-safe object type testing concise','Matching patterns in string fields','Testing object equality using patterns'],answer:1,explanation:'Pattern matching eliminates the cast anti-pattern: instead of "if (obj instanceof String) { String s = (String) obj; }", write "if (obj instanceof String s)" — s is in scope and typed without the explicit cast.'},
{id:840,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Project Loom" and "virtual threads" in Java (Java 21)?',options:['A UI threading framework','Lightweight threads (virtual threads) scheduled by the JVM rather than the OS, with near-zero creation/scheduling overhead — enabling millions of concurrent threads and simplifying concurrent programming without reactive or callback-based approaches','A loom for weaving class hierarchies','A framework for Java web applications'],answer:1,explanation:'Virtual threads (Java 21 stable): created with Thread.ofVirtual().start() or Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor(), they are mounted to OS platform threads as needed, enabling massive concurrency with simple blocking code.'},
{id:841,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between "String", "StringBuilder", and "StringBuffer" in Java?',options:['They are identical','String: immutable (every operation creates a new object); StringBuilder: mutable, NOT thread-safe (faster, single-threaded use); StringBuffer: mutable, thread-safe via synchronisation (slower, multi-threaded use)','StringBuilder is immutable; StringBuffer is mutable','StringBuffer is faster than StringBuilder'],answer:1,explanation:'String immutability: every concat creates a new object (performance problem in loops). StringBuilder: mutable, fast, not thread-safe — use in single-threaded contexts. StringBuffer: thread-safe (synchronised methods), use in multi-threaded contexts.'},
{id:842,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "method reference" in Java (Java 8+)?',options:['A reference to a deprecated method','A shorthand for a lambda that simply calls an existing method: ClassName::methodName or instance::methodName. Four forms: static (ClassName::staticMethod), instance-bound, instance-unbound, and constructor (ClassName::new)','A way to store method pointers','A reference to an abstract method in an interface'],answer:1,explanation:'Method references are more concise lambdas when the lambda body only calls an existing method: list.forEach(System.out::println) instead of list.forEach(s -> System.out.println(s)).'},
{id:843,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "var" (local variable type inference) in Java 10+?',options:['A variable that can change type at runtime','A keyword that lets the compiler infer the type of a local variable from its initialiser: var list = new ArrayList<String>() — the actual type is still statically determined at compile time','A variant type (like union type)','A volatile and reserved keyword'],answer:1,explanation:'var is a compile-time feature (not dynamic typing); the compiler infers the type. It only works for local variables with initialiser, for-each loop variables, and try-with-resources variables — not for fields, method parameters, or return types.'},
{id:844,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "instanceof" operator used for?',options:['Checking if a method exists on an object','Testing whether an object is an instance of a specified class or interface, returning true if so (including inherited types). Java 16+ adds pattern matching: if (obj instanceof String s)','Creating a new instance of a class','Comparing two object references'],answer:1,explanation:'instanceof checks type compatibility at runtime: useful before casting to avoid ClassCastException. Java 16 pattern matching: "if (shape instanceof Circle c)" combines the check and cast in one expression.'},
{id:845,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a "HashMap collision" and how does Java handle it?',options:['Two keys with the same value','When two different keys have the same hash code, they land in the same bucket; Java handles this with chaining (linked list/red-black tree in Java 8+ when chain length > 8) within the bucket','An error that crashes the HashMap','When two keys are equal'],answer:1,explanation:'Hash collisions are normal; Java 8+ HashMap uses a linked list for chains up to 8 entries, then converts to a red-black tree (TreeNode) for O(log n) worst case — preventing hash flooding attacks.'},
{id:846,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Java "ClassLoader" hierarchy?',options:['A class that loads images into memory','The three-level delegation model: Bootstrap ClassLoader (loads core Java classes from rt.jar/JDK modules) → Extension/Platform ClassLoader (loads extension modules) → Application ClassLoader (loads application classes from classpath) — each delegates to its parent first','A tool for loading classes from remote servers','A mechanism for hot-reloading classes'],answer:1,explanation:'ClassLoader hierarchy uses parent delegation: a ClassLoader always asks its parent to load first, then tries itself if the parent fails — preventing application code from overriding core JDK classes.'},
{id:847,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "reflection" in Java?',options:['A design pattern for mirroring class structures','The Java API allowing runtime inspection and manipulation of classes, methods, fields, constructors, and annotations — enabling frameworks (Spring, Hibernate) to create instances, invoke methods, and access fields dynamically without compile-time knowledge','A utility for comparing class structures','A debugging tool for viewing class metadata'],answer:1,explanation:'Reflection (java.lang.reflect): Class<?> cls = Class.forName("com.example.Foo"); cls.getDeclaredMethod("bar").invoke(instance, args). Powers dependency injection, ORM field mapping, and annotation processing at runtime.'},
{id:848,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the "Executor framework" and why is it preferred over raw Thread?',options:['An annotation processor framework','A high-level concurrency framework (java.util.concurrent) providing thread pools, task scheduling, and lifecycle management — preferred over raw Thread because it decouples task submission from execution policy, enabling pool reuse, bounded queues, and shutdown control','A framework for executing shell commands','A scheduler for Java batch jobs'],answer:1,explanation:'Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n) / newCachedThreadPool() provide managed thread pools; ExecutorService.submit(Callable) returns Future; ScheduledExecutorService handles periodic tasks — avoiding the overhead and management complexity of manual Thread creation.'},
{id:849,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "CompletableFuture" in Java 8+?',options:['A future that always completes successfully','An asynchronous computation that can be explicitly completed, chained with thenApply/thenCompose/thenCombine, and combined (allOf/anyOf), enabling non-blocking pipelines and explicit exception handling (exceptionally)','A synchronous Future implementation','A thread-safe future for multi-threaded use'],answer:1,explanation:'CompletableFuture enables async programming: chain thenApply (transform result), thenCompose (chain async stages), thenCombine (combine two futures), exceptionally (handle errors), all without blocking the calling thread.'},
{id:850,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "ConcurrentHashMap" and how does it achieve thread safety more efficiently than Hashtable?',options:['It is identical to Hashtable','Java 8+ ConcurrentHashMap uses node-level CAS (Compare-And-Swap) and synchronised blocks on individual bins rather than a global lock, allowing multiple threads to read and write different buckets concurrently — high concurrency vs Hashtable\'s single global lock','ConcurrentHashMap is always slower than Hashtable','ConcurrentHashMap prevents all concurrent access'],answer:1,explanation:'ConcurrentHashMap achieves high concurrency: reads are non-blocking, writes lock only the affected bin/node, enabling near-linear concurrency scaling vs Hashtable\'s single global lock serialising all operations.'},
{id:851,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the difference between "int" and "Integer" in Java?',options:['They are identical','int is a primitive type (stored directly on stack); Integer is a wrapper class (stored on heap as an object) that enables int to be used in collections, generic types, and with null values (Integer can be null, int cannot)','Integer is larger than int','int supports generics; Integer does not'],answer:1,explanation:'int: primitive (faster, no overhead). Integer: object wrapper enabling use in generics (List<Integer>), supporting null, and providing utility methods (Integer.parseInt, Integer.MAX_VALUE). Autoboxing converts between them.'},
{id:852,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does "public", "private", "protected", and "default" (package-private) access mean?',options:['They all mean the same thing','public: accessible everywhere; private: accessible only within the class; protected: accessible within the class, subclasses, and same package; default (no modifier): accessible only within the same package','protected means only accessible in child classes','private means accessible in all subclasses'],answer:1,explanation:'Access levels: public (all), protected (class + subclass + package), default/package-private (no modifier, class + package only), private (class only). Use the most restrictive access that still satisfies design requirements.'},
{id:853,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is an "enum" in Java?',options:['A counted number type','A special class type representing a fixed set of named constants, with optional fields, methods, and constructors — type-safe replacement for int constants','An enumeration of array elements','A list of class names'],answer:1,explanation:'Java enums are full-fledged classes: enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE } creates three singleton instances. Enums can have fields, constructors, and methods, and can implement interfaces.'},
{id:854,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a Java "package"?',options:['A jar file containing compiled classes','A namespace/grouping mechanism for related classes and interfaces, specified with the "package" declaration at the top of source files and used with "import" to access classes from other packages','A compressed archive of Java source code','A collection of Java methods'],answer:1,explanation:'Packages organise Java classes hierarchically (com.example.service), prevent naming conflicts, and control access (default access). The import statement brings specific classes or all classes from a package into scope.'},
{id:855,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a "JAR file" in Java?',options:['A file format for Java audio recordings','A Java ARchive file — a ZIP-format archive containing compiled class files, resources, and a MANIFEST.MF file, used to distribute and deploy Java libraries and applications','A configuration file for the JVM','A debug log file from the Java compiler'],answer:1,explanation:'JAR (Java ARchive) bundles compiled classes (.class), resources (images, properties), and manifest metadata into a single distributable file. Executable JARs include Main-Class in MANIFEST.MF.'},
{id:856,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "dependency injection" (DI) in Java and why is it important for testability?',options:['Importing external libraries into a class','Providing an object\'s dependencies from outside (via constructor, setter, or field injection) rather than having the object create them itself — making classes loosely coupled, independently testable with mocks/stubs, and configurable without code changes','Injecting SQL queries into a Java application','A security technique for preventing injection attacks'],answer:1,explanation:'DI externalises dependency creation: instead of new DatabaseService(), receive it via constructor parameter. This enables unit testing by injecting a mock/stub instead of a real dependency, and enables configuration (different implementations in test vs production).'},
{id:857,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "Spring Framework" used for?',options:['Building Android mobile applications','A comprehensive Java application framework providing dependency injection (IoC container), AOP, MVC web framework, data access abstraction, security, and more — simplifying enterprise Java development','A testing-only framework for Java','A Java IDE plugin'],answer:1,explanation:'Spring is the dominant Java enterprise framework: Spring Core (DI/IoC), Spring MVC (web), Spring Data (data access), Spring Security (auth), Spring Boot (convention-over-configuration rapid development).'},
{id:858,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "Spring Boot" and how does it differ from Spring Framework?',options:['A separate replacement for Spring Framework','Spring Boot provides auto-configuration, embedded server (Tomcat), starter dependencies, and production-ready features (Actuator) — reducing boilerplate configuration required by plain Spring Framework to get a production application running','Spring Boot only supports REST APIs','Spring Boot is a lighter version without DI'],answer:1,explanation:'Spring Boot is Spring with opinionated defaults: auto-configures beans based on classpath content, includes embedded Tomcat/Jetty, provides starter POMs for common use cases — enabling production-ready apps with minimal XML/Java config.'},
{id:859,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "Maven" used for in Java projects?',options:['Running Java unit tests','A build automation and project management tool that handles dependency management (downloading JARs from Maven Central), build lifecycle (compile, test, package, deploy), and project structure conventions','An IDE for Java development','A code review tool'],answer:1,explanation:'Maven manages: dependencies (pom.xml), build phases (validate, compile, test, package, install, deploy), plugins (Surefire for testing, Javadoc, Checkstyle), and project structure conventions (src/main/java, src/test/java).'},
{id:860,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between "Gradle" and "Maven"?',options:['They produce different output formats','Maven: XML-based (pom.xml), convention-over-configuration, declarative, less flexible. Gradle: Groovy/Kotlin DSL (build.gradle), highly flexible and customisable, incremental builds, faster for large projects','Gradle is only for Android','Maven supports more dependencies than Gradle'],answer:1,explanation:'Maven: stable, widely understood, verbose XML. Gradle: more flexible scripting (Groovy/Kotlin), incremental compilation and caching for faster builds, preferred in Android and many modern Java projects.'},
{id:861,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the "Observer design pattern" and how is it implemented in Java?',options:['A pattern for observing system performance','Defines a one-to-many dependency where multiple Observer objects register with a Subject/Observable; when the subject changes state, it notifies all registered observers. Implemented in Java with EventListener, java.util.Observer (deprecated), or custom interfaces','A pattern for monitoring thread execution','A design pattern for watching files for changes'],answer:1,explanation:'Observer pattern enables event-driven communication: Subject maintains a list of Observers (interface/callback), notifying all on state changes. Java implementations: PropertyChangeListener, custom event listener interfaces, or reactive streams (RxJava, Project Reactor).'},
{id:862,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the "Strategy design pattern"?',options:['A pattern for planning project strategy','Defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable — the client selects which strategy to use at runtime without changing the client code. Common in Java sort (Comparator), encryption, and payment processing','A pattern for managing multiple inheritance','A pattern for thread scheduling'],answer:1,explanation:'Strategy pattern: an interface defines the algorithm contract; multiple implementations provide different strategies; the context (client) holds a reference to a strategy interface and delegates. Sort: Arrays.sort(arr, comparator) uses Comparator as a strategy.'},
{id:863,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the "Singleton design pattern" and what is its thread-safe implementation in Java?',options:['A pattern for creating only one database connection','Ensures a class has only one instance and provides a global access point. Thread-safe implementations: eager initialisation (static final field), static inner class (Bill Pugh), or double-checked locking with volatile','A pattern for a list with one element','A class with only one method'],answer:1,explanation:'Thread-safe Singleton: (1) Eager init: private static final INSTANCE = new Singleton(); (2) Bill Pugh inner class: holder class loaded lazily on first getINSTANCE() call; (3) Enum: enum Singleton { INSTANCE } — JVM guarantees single enum instance.'},
{id:864,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the "Factory design pattern"?',options:['A factory class that produces database records','Defines an interface for creating objects without specifying concrete classes — the factory method is overridden in subclasses or configured externally to produce different object types, enabling decoupling of object creation from usage','A pattern for creating multiple instances of the same class','A pattern for managing database connections'],answer:1,explanation:'Factory pattern: ShapeFactory.create("circle") returns a Circle without the client knowing concrete class names. Abstract Factory: factory of factories for creating families of related objects. Enables swapping implementations without client code changes.'},
{id:865,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Java serialization" and what are its security implications?',options:['Converting objects to JSON for API calls','The process of converting an object\'s state to a byte stream (ObjectOutputStream) for storage or transmission, and deserialization (ObjectInputStream) reconstructs it. Security risk: deserializing untrusted data can trigger gadget chains leading to RCE (CVE-2015-4852, Apache Commons exploit)','The process of writing objects to XML','Converting String objects to byte arrays'],answer:1,explanation:'Java serialization is a critical security risk: deserialization of attacker-controlled bytes can trigger arbitrary code execution via gadget chains. Alternatives: JSON (Jackson, Gson), Protocol Buffers, or filtering with ObjectInputFilter.'},
{id:866,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation" in the JVM?',options:['Java source compilation happening just in time for deployment','The JVM compiling frequently-executed bytecode paths into native machine code at runtime, dramatically improving performance over pure interpretation — using profiling data (C1 client compiler for startup speed, C2 server compiler for peak throughput)','A compiler that checks syntax at runtime','Compilation triggered when the first test runs'],answer:1,explanation:'JIT compilation: the JVM identifies "hot methods" (frequently executed) and compiles them to native code. JVM uses tiered compilation: interpreted → C1 (fast compile, basic optimisation) → C2 (deep optimisation, inlining, escape analysis).'},
{id:867,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "escape analysis" in JVM optimisation?',options:['Detecting memory leaks that escape the heap','A JIT compiler analysis that determines whether an object\'s reference escapes the current method/thread; if not, the object can be stack-allocated or its synchronisation elided — improving performance by reducing heap allocations and lock overhead','Detecting objects that escape garbage collection','A security analysis for detecting resource leaks'],answer:1,explanation:'Escape analysis: if an object doesn\'t escape the creating method (local object, not returned, not stored in accessible field), JVM can stack-allocate it (avoiding GC), eliminate lock synchronisation (lock elision), or scalar-replace it.'},
{id:868,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Java Modules" (JPMS - Java Platform Module System, Java 9+)?',options:['A synonym for JAR files','A module system requiring explicit declaration of module dependencies (requires) and API exports (exports) in module-info.java, enabling strong encapsulation, reliable configuration, and smaller JVM runtime images (jlink)','A system for loading Maven modules','A feature for modular test execution'],answer:1,explanation:'JPMS (Project Jigsaw): modules declare explicit dependencies (requires module.x) and exposed packages (exports com.example.api), preventing split packages and enabling JVM runtime images containing only required modules (jlink).'},
{id:869,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "GraalVM native image" and how does it change Java deployment?',options:['A virtual machine for graphical Java applications','Ahead-of-time (AOT) compilation of Java applications into standalone native executables with sub-second startup time and lower memory footprint — eliminating the JVM startup overhead at the cost of dynamic features (reflection, proxies) requiring configuration','A faster JVM implementation','A tool for compiling Java to JavaScript'],answer:1,explanation:'GraalVM native-image AOT compiles to a native binary (includes classes + JVM subset); startup in milliseconds vs seconds, lower RSS — ideal for serverless/containerised workloads. Trade-off: longer build time, reflection/dynamic class loading needs configuration.'},
{id:870,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Project Panama" in Java?',options:['A Java project for network programming','A JDK project providing a foreign function and memory API (FFM API, finalized in Java 22) enabling Java programs to call native (C/C++) libraries and safely access off-heap memory without JNI\'s complexity and overhead','A Java enterprise application server project','A project for Java mobile development'],answer:1,explanation:'Panama\'s FFM API replaces JNI for calling native code: MethodHandles for foreign functions, MemorySegment for off-heap memory, VectorAPI for SIMD operations — safer, more performant, and far less complex than JNI.'},
{id:871,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the "this()" call in a Java constructor?',options:['Creating a new instance of the current class','Calling another constructor in the same class (constructor chaining), allowing one constructor to delegate to another with different parameters — must be the first statement in the constructor','Referring to the current class type','Calling the parent class constructor'],answer:1,explanation:'this() calls another constructor in the same class: constructor chaining reduces code duplication. this(defaultValue) from the no-arg constructor can delegate to a constructor requiring a parameter.'},
{id:872,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the "super()" call in a Java constructor?',options:['Creating an instance of the superclass','Calling the parent class constructor, explicitly passing arguments to the superclass initialisation code — must be the first statement in the child constructor (if not explicitly called, Java inserts a no-arg super() call automatically)','Calling a static method in the parent class','Accessing a parent class field'],answer:1,explanation:'super() explicitly invokes the parent class constructor with specified arguments. If no explicit super() is placed, Java automatically calls super() (no-arg parent constructor) — causing a compile error if the parent has no no-arg constructor.'},
{id:873,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the purpose of the "import" statement in Java?',options:['Loading compiled classes into memory','Allowing use of classes from other packages without their fully qualified name: import java.util.List; lets you write List instead of java.util.List in the code','Installing external libraries','Compiling external class files'],answer:1,explanation:'import makes type names available without full qualification. Wildcard import (import java.util.*) imports all types from the package but does not import sub-packages. Static import (import static) imports static members.'},
{id:874,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a "Java annotation"?',options:['A comment with extra syntax','Metadata that provides information about code to the compiler, tools, or runtime: @Override, @Deprecated, @SuppressWarnings, or custom annotations used by frameworks (Spring\'s @Autowired, JUnit\'s @Test)','A type of abstract class','A compile-time macro'],answer:1,explanation:'Annotations add metadata to classes, methods, fields, and parameters. The compiler uses them for validation (@Override), tools use them for code generation, and frameworks use them for configuration (Spring, Hibernate, JUnit).'},
{id:875,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is a "for-each" loop in Java?',options:['A loop that runs exactly five times','An enhanced for loop (for (Type element : collection)) that iterates over all elements of an array or Iterable without managing an index variable','A loop that runs until a condition is false','A method call on a collection'],answer:1,explanation:'Enhanced for-each: for (String s : list) { } — cleaner than indexed loops for full traversal of arrays and Iterable collections. Cannot be used when the index is needed or when modifying the collection.'},
{id:876,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "dependency injection" in the context of testing in Java?',options:['Injecting test data into a database','The practice of passing dependencies (services, repositories) to a class via constructor or setter, enabling test code to inject mock/stub implementations instead of real ones — making classes independently unit testable','Importing test frameworks into a class','A technique for test class inheritance'],answer:1,explanation:'DI makes unit testing trivial: inject a mock service instead of the real database-connected service. Without DI (if the class uses "new Service() " internally), tests require the real service, making them slow and brittle.'},
{id:877,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "Mockito" used for in Java testing?',options:['Mocking database records','A Java mocking framework that creates mock objects of classes or interfaces, stubs method return values, verifies interactions, and captures arguments — enabling isolated unit testing without real dependencies','A test runner for Java tests','A code coverage tool'],answer:1,explanation:'Mockito: mock(Service.class) creates a mock; when(mock.method()).thenReturn(value) stubs behaviour; verify(mock).method(any()) verifies interaction. Powers isolated unit testing alongside JUnit/TestNG.'},
{id:878,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Java Collections Framework?',options:['A framework for collecting user input','A unified architecture for representing and manipulating collections of objects, providing interfaces (List, Set, Queue, Map, Deque), implementations (ArrayList, HashMap, etc.), and algorithms (Collections.sort, shuffle)','A framework for database collection operations','A set of utility classes for Java streams'],answer:1,explanation:'JCF: interfaces define contracts (List, Set, Map, Queue); implementations provide specific data structures (ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, TreeMap, PriorityQueue); utility class Collections provides algorithms.'},
{id:879,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between "HashSet" and "TreeSet"?',options:['HashSet allows duplicates; TreeSet does not','HashSet: hash-based (O(1) add/contains, no order, allows one null); TreeSet: red-black tree (O(log n) add/contains, maintains sorted order per Comparator/natural ordering, no null in Java 8+)','TreeSet is always faster','HashSet maintains insertion order'],answer:1,explanation:'HashSet: fastest set operations, no ordering. TreeSet: maintains sorted order, enables first(), last(), headSet(), tailSet() range operations at O(log n) cost. LinkedHashSet maintains insertion order.'},
{id:880,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Java "Optional.orElseThrow()" method used for?',options:['Throwing a custom exception on null','Returns the value if present, or throws a specified exception (or NoSuchElementException if no supplier given) if the Optional is empty — clean handling of absent values without explicit null checks','Returning null when the Optional is empty','Throwing the Optional itself on error'],answer:1,explanation:'orElseThrow(ExceptionSupplier::new) retrieves the value or throws a supplied exception when empty, enabling clean fail-fast behaviour when a value is required to be present: userRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow(UserNotFoundException::new).'},
{id:881,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the "Command" design pattern in Java?',options:['The Java command-line argument parsing pattern','Encapsulates a request as an object, decoupling the sender from the receiver — enabling parameterisation of clients with different requests, queuing, logging, undoable operations. Implemented as: Command interface with execute()/undo(), ConcreteCommands, Invoker, Receiver','A pattern for processing command-line arguments','A pattern for executing SQL commands from Java'],answer:1,explanation:'Command pattern: GUI buttons each hold a Command object; undo/redo is implemented by stacking executed commands and calling undo(). Job queues, transaction logs, and macro recording use the Command pattern.'},
{id:882,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Decorator design pattern" in Java?',options:['A class with @Decorator annotation','Attaches additional responsibilities to an object dynamically at runtime without changing its class, by wrapping it in a Decorator object that adds behaviour before/after delegating to the wrapped object — Java I/O streams use this pattern extensively','A pattern for CSS-like styling of Java objects','A pattern for decorating exceptions with context'],answer:1,explanation:'Java I/O is the classic Decorator example: new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream(file)) wraps FileInputStream with buffering behaviour without changing FileInputStream. Each decorator adds behaviour while implementing the same interface.'},
{id:883,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Java Memory Management" zones: Eden, Survivor, Old Generation?',options:['Database memory zones','Heap generations in Java GC: Eden (new objects allocated here), Survivor (S0/S1: objects surviving Minor GC), Old Generation (Tenured: objects surviving many Minor GCs). Minor GC (Young Gen), Major/Full GC (Old Gen or full heap)','Network buffer zones in NIO','Memory zones in the Java module system'],answer:1,explanation:'Generational GC: Eden (fast alloc), Survivor spaces (objects promoted after surviving GC cycles), Old Gen (long-lived objects). Minor GC is fast (Young Gen only); Full GC is slow (entire heap). GC tuning controls promotion thresholds and generation sizes.'},
{id:884,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a "WeakReference" in Java and when is it used?',options:['A reference to a small object','A reference that does not prevent garbage collection of the referenced object; the GC can collect the object even if a WeakReference exists — used for caches (WeakHashMap), preventing memory leaks for listeners/callbacks, and reference equality in sets','A reference from a subclass to a superclass','A null-safe reference wrapper'],answer:1,explanation:'WeakReference: if the only references to an object are weak references, the GC can collect it. WeakHashMap uses weak keys — entries are automatically removed when the key becomes weakly reachable. Prevents memory leaks in caches and listener patterns.'},
{id:885,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "bytecode instrumentation" in Java?',options:['Adding comments to bytecode','Modifying compiled Java bytecode (.class files) at load-time or post-compilation to add behavior (logging, performance monitoring, aspect weaving, code coverage) without changing source code — used by Spring AOP, JaCoCo, Byte Buddy, ASM','Encrypting Java bytecode for distribution','Compiling bytecode to native machine code'],answer:1,explanation:'Bytecode instrumentation powers: JaCoCo code coverage (inserts probes at bytecode level), Spring AOP (Cglib/ByteBuddy proxy generation), profiling agents, and aspect-oriented programming frameworks.'},
{id:886,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the Java "Scanner" class used for?',options:['Scanning source code for bugs','Reading input from various sources (System.in, files, strings) with methods like nextInt(), nextLine(), nextDouble() — commonly used for reading user input in console applications','Scanning JAR files for dependencies','A code style scanner'],answer:1,explanation:'Scanner wraps an InputStream or Reader to provide convenient parsing methods: sc.nextLine() reads a whole line, sc.nextInt() reads and parses an int, etc. Don\'t forget to close with sc.close() or use try-with-resources.'},
{id:887,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does System.out.println() do in Java?',options:['Reads input from the console','Prints a string representation of the argument to standard output (console), followed by a newline character','Writes to a file','Logs a message to a log file'],answer:1,explanation:'System.out is the standard output PrintStream. println() calls toString() on the argument and writes to stdout with a newline. System.out.print() writes without newline; System.err.println() writes to stderr.'},
{id:888,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is an "array" in Java?',options:['A collection class in java.util','A fixed-size container holding elements of the same type, accessed by zero-based integer index. Arrays are objects in Java: int[] arr = new int[10]; String[] names = new String[5]','A dynamic list that grows automatically','A key-value storage structure'],answer:1,explanation:'Arrays: fixed size set at creation, all elements same type, zero-based indexed access (arr[0]), have .length property. Use ArrayList for dynamic sizing. int[] and int[][] (multi-dimensional) are both valid array types.'},
{id:889,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does "Arrays.sort()" do in Java?',options:['Creates a sorted copy of the array','Sorts the specified array in ascending natural order using a dual-pivot Quicksort (primitives) or TimSort (objects), modifying the original array in place','Searches for an element in an array','Converts an array to a sorted List'],answer:1,explanation:'Arrays.sort(arr) sorts the array in-place (modifies it). For object arrays, it uses TimSort (stable). For primitives, dual-pivot Quicksort. Arrays.sort(arr, comparator) sorts with a custom ordering.'},
{id:890,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the "ternary operator" in Java?',options:['An operator working on three arrays','condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse — a compact inline alternative to an if-else expression for simple conditional assignments','An operator that performs three operations simultaneously','A bitwise operator affecting three bits'],answer:1,explanation:'The ternary operator: int max = (a > b) ? a : b; is equivalent to: if (a > b) { max = a; } else { max = b; } — useful for concise conditional expressions in assignments and method returns.'},
{id:891,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "try-catch-finally" in Java exception handling?',options:['A type of conditional statement','try: contains code that might throw an exception. catch: handles specific exception types. finally: always executes (cleanup code) regardless of whether an exception was thrown or caught — even if a return statement is in try or catch','A loop construct for retrying operations','A database transaction construct'],answer:1,explanation:'try-catch-finally: the finally block always runs (close connections, release resources) — used as a cleanup guarantee. Note: if try-with-resources is available, it is preferred over finally for resource cleanup.'},
{id:892,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "instanceof" pattern matching with "switch" in Java 21?',options:['A pattern for switching between database instances','Java 21 switch expressions allow patterns: switch (obj) { case Integer i -> ...; case String s when s.length() > 5 -> ...; default -> ... } — enabling type-safe, exhaustive multi-type dispatch','A pattern for switching thread context','A feature for switching class loaders'],answer:1,explanation:'Java 21 switch patterns enable type-test-and-bind in switch expressions/statements, replacing cascading instanceof chains. Guarded patterns (case String s when s.isEmpty()) add conditions. Sealed type exhaustiveness is compiler-checked.'},
{id:893,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is "Java I/O" NIO.2 (java.nio.file)?',options:['Non-blocking I/O for network sockets only','A comprehensive file system API (Java 7+) providing Path, Files, FileSystem classes for file operations with better exception handling, symbolic link support, file watching (WatchService), and attribute access than the older java.io.File','A replacement for all Java I/O','A file API for Java mobile applications'],answer:1,explanation:'NIO.2 (java.nio.file): Path (immutable file path), Files (static utility: read, write, copy, move, walk), FileSystem, WatchService (file change notifications). Preferred over legacy java.io.File for new code.'},
{id:894,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between "Comparable" and "Comparator" for sorting?',options:['Comparable is for primitives; Comparator for objects','Comparable: the class implements it (natural ordering: a.compareTo(b)); Comparator: external comparison logic (compare(a,b)) passed to sort methods, enabling multiple orderings without modifying the class. Use Comparator.comparing() for lambda-based comparators','Comparator is faster','Comparable compares strings; Comparator compares numbers'],answer:1,explanation:'Collections.sort(list) uses Comparable (natural order); Collections.sort(list, Comparator.comparing(Person::getName)) uses an external Comparator. Multiple Comparators chain with .thenComparing() for multi-level sorts.'},
{id:895,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Project Amber" in Java evolution?',options:['An amber-colored Java IDE theme','A JDK project focusing on small, productivity-oriented Java language features: records (JDK 16), sealed classes (JDK 17), text blocks (JDK 15), pattern matching for instanceof (JDK 16), pattern matching for switch (JDK 21), and more','A database connectivity project','A Java performance improvement project'],answer:1,explanation:'Project Amber delivers language improvements making Java more expressive: records eliminate boilerplate data classes, sealed classes enable algebraic types, pattern matching replaces instanceof cascades, text blocks improve multi-line strings.'},
{id:896,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "structural typing" and how do Java interfaces provide a form of it?',options:['Typing based on physical code structure','Structural typing matches types based on structure (having the same methods) rather than nominal type relationships. Java uses nominal typing (must explicitly declare "implements Interface") but interfaces provide a contract-based abstraction similar to structural typing','A typing system based on class hierarchy','An annotation-based type check'],answer:1,explanation:'Java is nominally typed (explicit type declarations required), but interfaces enable behaviour abstraction: unrelated classes implementing the same interface are polymorphically interchangeable, achieving similar benefits to structural typing for defined contracts.'},
{id:897,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "text block" in Java (Java 15+)?',options:['A large block of text in a GUI component','A multi-line string literal delimited by triple quotes (""") that preserves formatting, eliminates most escape sequences, and strips consistent leading whitespace — ideal for JSON, SQL, HTML, and XML literal strings in code','A block of text read from a file','A StringBuilder with text formatting methods'],answer:1,explanation:'Text blocks: String json = """ { "name": "Bob" } """; — incidental whitespace is stripped, newlines preserved, no escape sequences needed for quotes/newlines. stripIndent() and formatted() are companion methods.'},
{id:898,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "structured concurrency" in Java (Java 21, preview)?',options:['Organizing threads in a class hierarchy','A concurrency API treating a group of related tasks as a single unit of work: if any subtask fails, others are cancelled; all subtasks complete before the parent completes — preventing thread leak, error propagation ambiguity, and simplifying cancellation','A structure for synchronizing threads','A concurrent data structure'],answer:1,explanation:'Structured Concurrency (StructuredTaskScope): ensures all forked threads complete (or are cancelled) before the scope exits, making concurrent code easier to reason about — eliminating the "escaped thread" problem of unstructured ExecutorService usage.'},
{id:899,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "method handle" in Java (java.lang.invoke)?',options:['A handle to a deprecated method','A typed, directly executable reference to a method, constructor, or field that is more efficient than reflection and supports dynamic invocation with invoke() and invokeExact() — used by invokedynamic for lambda/method reference implementation and dynamic language support','A wrapper for legacy methods','A pointer to a native JNI method'],answer:1,explanation:'MethodHandle (Java 7+): low-overhead method references for dynamic invocation. invoke() does type conversion; invokeExact() requires exact types. The JVM\'s lambda and method reference implementation (LambdaMetafactory) uses invokedynamic + MethodHandles.'},
{id:900,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the "double-checked locking" pattern for Singleton in Java and why does it require volatile?',options:['A pattern for checking locks twice for performance','A lazy Singleton initialisation pattern: check if instance is null (first check, no lock), acquire lock, check again (second check, with lock), then initialise. The volatile keyword on the instance field prevents the JVM from reordering the object\'s partial construction with the assignment, ensuring all threads see the fully initialised object','A debugging pattern for detecting deadlocks','A pattern for two-phase commit in transactions'],answer:1,explanation:'Without volatile, the JVM (or CPU) may publish a partially-initialised reference: after "new Singleton()" allocates memory and assigns the reference but before the constructor completes, another thread might see a non-null but uninitialised instance.'},
{id:901,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the output of: System.out.println(10 / 3) in Java?',options:['3.33','3','3.0','Error'],answer:1,explanation:'Integer division in Java truncates toward zero: 10 / 3 = 3 (integer, discards remainder). For floating-point division, use: 10.0 / 3 or (double)10 / 3.'},
{id:902,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the output of: System.out.println("5" + 2 + 3) in Java?',options:['523','10','55','Error'],answer:0,explanation:'String concatenation is left-to-right: "5" + 2 = "52" (String+int = String), then "52" + 3 = "523". To get 10: System.out.println("5" + (2 + 3)) or Integer.parseInt("5") + 2 + 3.'},
{id:903,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What keyword prevents a class from being subclassed?',options:['static','abstract','final','private'],answer:2,explanation:'A final class cannot be extended: final class String {...}. String and Integer are final Java classes. Final ensures the class\'s behaviour cannot be changed through subclassing.'},
{id:904,topic:'java',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What is the default value of an int field in a Java class?',options:['null','-1','undefined','0'],answer:3,explanation:'Java initialises instance/class fields to default values: int/long/short/byte → 0, float/double → 0.0, boolean → false, char → \'\\u0000\', object references → null. Local variables have no default (must be explicitly initialised).'},
{id:905,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the "Iterable" interface and how does it relate to the for-each loop?',options:['A marker interface for collection classes','An interface with one abstract method iterator() that returns an Iterator<T>; any class implementing Iterable can be used in an enhanced for-each loop','A type that can be converted to an Iterator','An interface only for List implementations'],answer:1,explanation:'The Java compiler desugars for (T t : iterable) to: Iterator<T> it = iterable.iterator(); while(it.hasNext()) { T t = it.next(); ... }. Implementing Iterable enables for-each loop use on custom classes.'},
{id:906,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a "functional interface" predicate in Java?',options:['A predicate used in SQL WHERE clauses','java.util.function.Predicate<T> is a functional interface with test(T t) → boolean, used in stream.filter(pred), List.removeIf(pred), and composition (and, or, negate) — representing boolean-valued conditions','A predicate for type checking','An interface for database query predicates'],answer:1,explanation:'Predicate<T>: test(T t) returns boolean. Stream.filter(s -> s.length() > 5) uses a Predicate<String>. Predicates compose: Predicate<String> combined = isLong.and(isUpperCase).negate().'},
{id:907,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between "map" and "flatMap" in Java Streams?',options:['They are identical','map applies a 1-to-1 transformation function to each element, returning a Stream of the transformed values; flatMap applies a 1-to-many transformation that returns a Stream per element, then flattens all streams into a single Stream','flatMap filters; map transforms','map is for Optional; flatMap is for Streams'],answer:1,explanation:'map: Stream<String> → apply length() → Stream<Integer> (1:1). flatMap: Stream<List<String>> → apply Collection::stream → Stream<String> (flattens nested streams). Use flatMap to flatten nested structures.'},
{id:908,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the "Collectors.groupingBy()" method do?',options:['Groups test classes by annotation','A collector that groups Stream elements by a classifier function into a Map<K, List<V>> — enabling SQL GROUP BY-like operations: Collectors.groupingBy(Person::getDepartment) produces a Map<String, List<Person>>','Groups imports in source files','Groups elements in a sorted order'],answer:1,explanation:'groupingBy(classifier): groups stream elements by the return value of classifier into a Map. With a downstream collector: Collectors.groupingBy(Person::getDept, Collectors.counting()) maps department to count.'},
{id:909,topic:'java',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the Java "Executor" interface and ExecutorService?',options:['An interface for executing database commands','Executor has a single execute(Runnable) method. ExecutorService extends Executor, adding: submit(Callable) → Future, shutdown, awaitTermination, invokeAll, invokeAny — providing lifecycle management for thread pool tasks','A tool for executing shell commands','An annotation for scheduled methods'],answer:1,explanation:'ExecutorService (Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)) manages a thread pool; submit(callable) returns Future for async result retrieval; shutdown() initiates orderly shutdown; awaitTermination() blocks until tasks complete.'},
{id:910,topic:'java',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is "Java Flight Recorder" (JFR) and when is it used?',options:['A test result recording tool','A low-overhead (< 1% overhead) profiling and diagnostics engine built into the JDK that records JVM events (GC, JIT, thread states, exception rates, method profiling) into binary .jfr files for analysis with Java Mission Control (JMC) or other tools','A code coverage recorder','A log file recorder for Java applications'],answer:1,explanation:'JFR is production-safe profiling: continuous recording with minimal overhead enables always-on diagnostics. Captures: memory allocation rates, GC pauses, lock contention, CPU hotspots, thread dumps, exception frequencies. Replaces many separate monitoring tools.'}
]);
